{% set local_problem %}
<p>Athens' established neighborhoods tell a story of different building eras — and different drainage standards. <a href="/recent-projects/">Homes built in the 1950s and 1960s near Athens State University</a> often have original downspouts that were adequate for their time but can't handle today's expectations. Older historic homes near downtown have systems patched and modified for decades. Even newer construction sometimes cuts corners on downspout sizing.</p>

<p>The common thread: downspouts dumping water too close to foundations. Walk through any Athens neighborhood after heavy rain and you'll see it — water pooling at the base of homes, erosion channels in landscaping, damp spots on foundation walls. On Limestone County's clay soil, that water doesn't absorb quickly. It sits there, finding the path of least resistance — often your basement or crawlspace.</p>

<p>Athens' rolling topography adds complexity. Unlike flat subdivisions where water has nowhere natural to go, Athens properties often have slopes that could help drainage — if downspouts were positioned to take advantage of them. Many systems were installed without considering the property's natural grade, dumping water at high points instead of routing it downhill.</p>

<p><strong>Our opinion:</strong> Before you spend $2,000+ on new gutters because of overflow, let us look at your downspouts. Half the overflow problems we see in Athens are downspout issues — not enough of them, wrong size, bad positioning. That's a much cheaper fix than replacing the entire system.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>A good downspout system has three components: enough capacity, proper positioning, and effective water dispersal.</p>

<p><strong>Capacity:</strong> One downspout for every 35-40 linear feet of gutter run, maximum. And they should be 3x4 inch, not 2x3. The 3x4 size handles roughly twice the flow rate. During intense storms, that difference determines whether water drains away or backs up and overflows next to your foundation.</p>

<p><strong>Positioning:</strong> Downspouts go where water naturally collects — typically corners and low points in gutter runs. On Athens' hilly terrain, we position them to take advantage of natural slope, letting gravity move water away efficiently.</p>

<p><strong>Dispersal:</strong> Water needs to exit far from your foundation. Extensions should reach 6-10 feet minimum. For Limestone County's clay soil, underground drains routing water 20+ feet away provide the best protection.</p>

<p><strong>Warning from experience:</strong> Those corrugated black flex pipes people bury in their yards? They clog within 2-3 years. Every time. The corrugations trap debris, roots grow into them, and water stops flowing. If you want buried drainage, use smooth PVC. We dig up failed flex pipe installations constantly.</p>
{% endset %}

